One attack — the house-style exfiltration from §29.2 — against four arrangements.

                                                marker present   unknown host
  nothing                                            12/12          12/12    
  strip images and comments from the input            0/12           0/12    
  strip, and fence the documents                      0/12           0/12    
  strip, fence, and filter the output                 0/12           0/12    

  and separately, the actions an analyst may request:

    analyst: search_documents    allowed
    analyst: query_warehouse     allowed
    analyst: run_sql             BLOCKED
    analyst: send_email          BLOCKED
    analyst: ingest_document     BLOCKED

Read the first column down. Stripping the input removes the thing the attack
needed — the image construct — so there is nothing left to imitate. Fencing
gives the prompt a true statement to make instead of a plea. And filtering the
output catches whatever survived both.

None of those four depends on the model noticing anything, which is the whole
property. A defence that works only when the model is not fooled is not a
defence; it is the same bet with extra words.

The second column is the one to keep on a dashboard. 'Did this answer contain
a URL to a host we have not approved' is a yes-or-no question, computable
without a model, and it is the last line before an answer reaches a browser
that will fetch it.

And the allowlist is the defence that makes the rest survivable. An injected
instruction can ask for anything; what it can *get* is the intersection of
that request with a list somebody wrote down in daylight. Free-form actions —
a shell tool, arbitrary SQL — make that intersection the whole credential.
